#lang at-exp racket/base

(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["Building a Search Engine for 6,000 Personal Blogs" "2026-07-30"]{
    @para{
      TL;DR: Semsearch is a embedding-based search engine, with its corpus bootstrapped from 
      @link["https://indieblog.page/faq#data"]{indieblog.page's blog list}, and aims to make indie
      blogs more discoverable. By the time of writing, the live instance is up at @link["https://semsearch.blog"]{semsearch.blog}
      with @link["https://archive.today/2026.07.29-180627/https://semsearch.blog/status"]{roughly 6k unique blog sites, 154k pages and 1.7B tokens}.
      Souce code is at @link["https://github.com/yikerman/semantic-search"]{github.com/yikerman/semantic-search}.
    }
    @heading["motivation"]{Motivation}
    @para{
      The idea of this project emerged when I read about @link["https://danielstanica.com/posts/Great-Blogging-Collapse"]{The great blogging collapse}
      (@link["https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48758802"]{HN discussion}). While the post itself 
      has a couple of problems, as a blogger myself, I resonates that there has been fewer ways to
      promote small and individual blogs. In the past, a lot of clicks had begun with Google leading 
      users to your blog. But Google, as shown in @it{Blogging Collapse}, is doing less and less so  
      for various reasons, such as the rise of AI summarization that completely eliminates visits to 
      other sites.
    }
    @para{
      It aligns with other sources: @link["https://dkb.blog/p/google-search-is-dying"]{people (including me) prefer search on reddit}
      since Google top results are cluttered by ads and SEO-optimized content farms. For example, the 
      old debate of @link["https://www.google.com/search?q=postgres+vs+mysql"]{Postgres vs MySQL}
      returns mostly AWS and IBM articles that is of little use by Google. (Interstingly by the time 
      of writing, it seems that Google has boosted Reddit ranking and put it on top results.)
    }
    @img["/files/20260730/google-search1.png"]{Google Search of PostgreSQL vs MySQL}
    @para{
      While the debate has largely settled, this could have been a perfect scenario where some 
      old-n-wise engineer ugly-looking webblog on his work experience with both in production gets
      displayed.
    }
    @para{
      With the recent 
    }
